[code snippet 1]
```java
import java.util.Date;
import java.text.SimpleDateFormat;

public class InsecureDateFormat {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SimpleDateFormat sdf = new SimpleDateFormat("MM-dd-yyyy");
        String dateString = "01-01-2022";
        Date date = sdf.parse(dateString);
        System.out.println("Date: " + date);
    }
}
```
In this example, the `SimpleDateFormat` class is used to parse a date string, but it does not perform any input validation, making it vulnerable to `FormatException` attacks. An attacker can easily manipulate the date string to cause a `FormatException` and potentially crash the application or execute arbitrary code.

[code snippet 2]
```java
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;
import java.time.temporal.Chronology;

public class SecureDateFormat {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DateTimeFormatter sdf = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("MM-dd-yyyy");
        String dateString = "01-01-2022";
        try {
            TemporalType temporalType = Chronology.getInstance().date(sdf.parse(dateString));
            System.out.println("Date: " + temporalType);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid date format: " + e);
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `DateTimeFormatter` class is used to parse a date string, which performs input validation and is less vulnerable to `FormatException` attacks. The `Chronology` class is used to handle the date in a more secure manner.

The vulnerable code snippet is:
```
Date date = sdf.parse(dateString);
```
The secure code snippet is:
```
TemporalType temporalType = Chronology.getInstance().date(sdf.parse(dateString));
```

